# Paragraph-query gold — confluence's NATIVE regime (PRD critical note: "a long
# paragraph ... each sentence lights up different parts ... the parts that light up
# the most" win). Each query is a multi-sentence paragraph whose sentences converge
# on ONE answer region. key_facts are reused verbatim from eval/gold.jsonl (same,
# pre-validated answers) so this isolates paragraph framing → a controlled sum-vs-max
# (confluence on/off) test. id maps to the single-sentence source query.
{"id": "p01", "source": "g01", "hard": false, "query": "I keep coming back to how advertising shapes the prices I pay. Every product seems to have layers of marketing cost baked into it. And the companies that win are the ones with the best ads, not the best products. Why did I conclude advertisements are bad for the economy?", "key_facts": ["advertising forms an economic bubble adding cost without value", "product priced x+ny where n=ads in loop and y=ad cost", "sellers favor great ads over great products, cutting product quality/research"]}
{"id": "p03", "source": "g03", "hard": true, "query": "I have been reflecting on where founders go wrong with network effects. So many assume that simply getting users to invite their friends creates a flywheel. Some even pay influencers to drag their audiences onto a platform before there is anything worth using. What did I get wrong about network effects that most founders also miss?", "key_facts": ["must offer value to individuals before any network exists", "unhappy customers never lead to network effects", "paying influencers to bring their network is a waste"]}
{"id": "p05", "source": "g05", "hard": true, "query": "I keep thinking about why work feels so coercive to most people. They take jobs they hate just to afford rent and food each month. It all seems tied to who controls land and the natural resources we are born needing. Why do I argue modern employment is a form of slavery, and what is the alternative?", "key_facts": ["natural resources cornered by a privileged few forces people into jobs to survive", "parasitic vs symbiotic systems; symbiosis = voluntary mutual exchange of values", "everyone should have access to natural resources by birth"]}
{"id": "p06", "source": "g06", "hard": false, "query": "I have been mapping out where AI goes next. The frontier models themselves are commoditising fast and the weights are catching up to each other. What seems to create real lock-in is whether the system remembers you across sessions. What do I believe is the next paradigm shift in AI?", "key_facts": ["memory is the next paradigm", "memory creates switching cost which becomes the moat for monetisation", "current snippet/chat memories may be insufficient vs hippocampus-like processing"]}
{"id": "p11", "source": "g11", "hard": true, "query": "I have been tracing how my views on religion hardened over the years. I used to simply not believe in any god. Now I actively think faith is harmful, like handing control of your life to an external agency. Why have I grown from atheist to anti-theist about religion?", "key_facts": ["religion establishes an external agency with control/agency over one's life", "giving up that agency hides from reality and cripples growth", "analogy: a crutch weakening healthy legs / a crippling addiction"]}
{"id": "p12", "source": "g12", "hard": true, "query": "I keep turning over the ethics of gig platforms. Drivers sign up freely but cannot really refuse the terms once they depend on the income. They get deactivated without warning, work without rest breaks, and often earn below minimum wage. What is my argument that gig work is coercive labour, and how could it be done better?", "key_facts": ["gig contract is legally voluntary but economically coercive", "issues: skirts minimum wage, no rest, no unionising, arbitrary deactivation acts as firing", "better models exist: Domino's salaried riders, Shadowfax minimum-guarantee"]}
{"id": "p13", "source": "g13", "hard": false, "query": "I have been thinking about what actually makes farming sustainable. Part of it is the farmer's livelihood, part of it is the health of the land, and part of it is the economics of cultivation. Natural farming keeps coming up as the way to hold all three together. What does sustainable agriculture mean to me and why am I bullish on natural farming?", "key_facts": ["three aspects: social, economical, environmental", "sustainability comes from reducing the inherent risk in agriculture", "ZBNF / natural farming cuts cost of cultivation while keeping yield comparable to chemical farming"]}
{"id": "p14", "source": "g14", "hard": false, "query": "I have been building a mental model of why systems collapse. It seems to happen when something changes too fast for the system to adapt to it. The resilient ones stay in tune with their environment and absorb shocks instead of shattering. What is my framework for why systems fail and how to make them resilient?", "key_facts": ["resilience rooted in a system staying in sync with its surroundings", "extreme change without feedback loops breaks the system", "elasticity / ability to absorb shocks and evolve = resilience/anti-fragility"]}
